          %h2#the_canonical_model_structure_on_
            The canonical model structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure"} model structure
            which encapsulates part of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            as a version of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on categorical structures, and is also called the
            %strong trivial model structure
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong categorical model structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Cat%29"} Ho(Cat)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            by identifying naturally isomorphic functors. See the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Model+structures+on+Cat"} Catlab
            for the theory of this structure.
          %p
            Assuming the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %em unique
            model structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            such that the weak equivalences are categorical equivalences (thus justifying the word ‘canonical’). It is different from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
          %p
            On this page we give a concise construction of the canonical model structure, as well as two variants that make sense in the absence of the full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h3#the_classical_version The classical version
          %p
            For purposes of this page,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            will denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            as in ordinary set-theoretic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            for the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
          #ClassesOfMorphisms.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p Declare a functor to be:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
                  if it is an
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
                %p
                  (equivalently:
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
                  and
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration"} cofibration
                  if it is
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective-on-objects+functor"} injective on objects
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isocofibration"} isocofibration
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                  if it is an
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
            %p
          %p
          #ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The classes in Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassesOfMorphisms"}
              constitute a
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi can
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{can}
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %br/
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              It is easy to verify that the weak equivalences satisfy the
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-3+property"} 2-out-of-3 property
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
            %p Suppose given a square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; C\\
                    ^i\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^p\\
                    B&amp; \underset{g}{\to} &amp; D}
            %p
              in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is a cofibration and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              a fibration.
            %p
              Suppose first that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is acyclic. It is easy to see that the acyclic fibrations are precisely the equivalences of categories that are surjective on objects. Thus, since (mono, epi) is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system+on+Set"} weak factorization system on Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_0\colon B_0\to C_0
              filling the square, and then full-faithfulness of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              gives a unique definition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              on arrows.
            %p
              Now suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is acyclic, so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0 \colon A_0 \to B_0
              is injective. Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is essentially surjective, we can choose, for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∖
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B_0 \setminus A_0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_b\colon i(a_b) \cong b
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\phi_b)\colon p(f(a_b)) = g(i(a_b)) \cong g(b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is an isofibration, we can also choose, for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_b\colon f(a_b) \cong c_b
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\psi_b) = g(\phi_b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_0\colon B_0\to C_0
              to be
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
              on the image of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0
              and to take
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∖
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B_0 \setminus A_0
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              on arrows by composing with the isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
              to make it a lifting.
            %p
              It remains to prove the factorization axioms. Suppose given a functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 = A_0 + B_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              into a category in the unique way such that the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to B
              induced by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_B
              is fully faithful. Since it is surjective on objects, it is an acyclic fibration, and clearly the induced map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to C
              is injective on objects, i.e. a cofibration.
            %p
              Next, define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              to be the category of triples
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b,\phi)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon f(a)\cong b
              is an isomorphism in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} strict iso-comma category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f/_\cong 1_b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to B
              is easily shown to be an isofibration, while the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to D
              defined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mapsto (a, f(a), 1_{f(a)})
              is an injective equivalence.
            %p
              This completes the proof.
              %br/
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The two factorizations constructed above are in fact
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+factorizations"} functorial factorizations
            %p
              While this model structure is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrantly+generated+model+category"} cofibrantly generated
              (Prop.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CofibrantGeneration"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
              (though they are closely related to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+systems"} algebraic weak factorization systems
              produced by the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+small+object+argument"} algebraic small object argument
          %h3#without_choice Without choice
          %p
            In the absence of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %em strong equivalences of categories
            = succeed "," do
              %em weak equivalences of categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            whose weak equivalences are the
            %em weak
            equivalences of categories. The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            also suggests such an approach, since an anafunctor (the “right” replacement for a functor in the absence of choice) is a particular sort of generalized morphism: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\leftarrow F \to B
            of functors in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to A
            is a surjective equivalence.
          %p
            If there is to be such a model structure, however, then since generalized morphisms between fibrant-and-cofibrant objects are all represented by ordinary ones, there must exist “cofibrant categories” and “fibrant categories” such that every anafunctor between fibrant-and-cofibrant categories is equivalent to an honest functor, and every category can be replaced by a fibrant and cofibrant one. It seems unlikely that this would be true without
            %em any
            choice-like axioms, but notably weaker axioms than full AC do suffice.
          %h4#the_projective_model_structure The projective model structure
          %p
            For the existence of the model structure in this case, we assume
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            has enough
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
            to be:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
                if it is a weak equivalence of categories, i.e.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective on objects
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration"} cofibration
                if it is injective on objects, and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0\setminus f(A_0)
                is a projective object in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                if it is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
          %p
            As before, the acyclic fibrations are precisely the weak equivalences that are literally surjective on objects. Now recall that assuming COSHEP, (monics with projective complement, epics) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system+on+Set"} weak factorization system on Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
            into a cofibration followed by an acyclic fibration is given by first factoring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 \to A_0 + B_0' \to B_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0'\to B_0
            is a projective cover.
          %p
            The other factorization works exactly as before, while for lifting acyclic cofibrations against fibrations, we notice that in the original proof, we only needed to apply choice for sets indexed by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0\setminus i(A_0)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon A\to B
            is a cofibration.
          %p Weak equivalences of categories are easily seen to satisfy the 2-out-of-3 property, so we have a model category. Note that all categories are fibrant in this model structure, while the cofibrant categories are those whose set of objects is projective.
          %p
            The existence of this model structure implies, in particular, that under COSHEP the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ana
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D)
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+small+category"} essentially small
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(C',D)
            of ordinary functors where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            is a cofibrant replacement for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h4#the_injective_model_structure The injective model structure
          %p
            Is there a dual model structure in which all categories are cofibrant? This seemingly has to do with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            completion: the fibrant objects would be
            %em stacks
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+coverage"} regular coverage
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+small+cardinality+selection"} axiom of small cardinality selection
            (which he uses, instead of COSHEP, to prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ana
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D)
            is essentially small) sufficient for the existence of an “injective” model structure on Cat?
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#BasicProperties Basic properties
          %p
          #CategoryOfBifibrantObjects.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (category of bifibrant objects)
              %br/
              It is obvious that all objects in the canonical model structure (Prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibrant+object"} bifibrant
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  both
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+object"} cofibrant
                %p
                  since a functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∅
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to \mathcal{C}
                  from the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+category"} empty category
                  is evidently an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injective function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∅
                        %mo →
                        %mi Obj
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to Obj(\mathcal{C})
                  on objects,
              %li
                %p
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant
                %p
                  since a functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo →
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \to \ast
                  to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
                  is evidently an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
                  since the only morphism in the terminal category to lift is an
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %p
              Hence, in particular,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi can
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{can}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
              and a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration+category"} cofibration category
          %p
          #TheAcyclicFibrations.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (acyclic (co)fibrations)
              %br/
              In the canonical model structure (of Prop.
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure"}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibrations"} acyclic cofibrations
                  are precisely the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective-on-objects+functor"} injective-on-objects
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
                  are precisely the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective-on-objects+functor"} surjective on objects
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
                %p
                  Namely, being
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
                  makes them be
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
                  (
                  %a{:href => "equivalence+of+categories#ViaSplitEssentiallySurjectiveAndFullyFaithful"}> this Prop.
                  ), but being also
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibrations"} isofibrations
                  then clearly requires them to actually be
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+on+objects+functor"} surjective on objects
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithfulness
                  is then already sufficient for isofibrancy.
            %p
          %p
          #Properness.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (properness)
              %br/
              The canonical model structure (Prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} proper model category
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              In view of Rem.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfBifibrantObjects"}
              this follows by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "proper+model+category#AllObjectsFibrantImpliesRightProper"} this Prop.
          %p
          #CofibrantGeneration.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (cofibrant generation)
              %br/
              The canonical model structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi can
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{can}
              (Prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrantly+generated+model+category"} cofibrantly generated
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              of generating cofibrations and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              of generating acyclic cofibrations may be taken to consist of the following evident functors:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "center center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∅
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo ⊔
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo →
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ⇉
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo →
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    I
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left\{
                    \\begin{array}{ccc}
                    \\varnothing &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \{ 0 \}
                    \\,,
                    \\\
                    \\{0\} \sqcup \{1\} &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \{ 0 \to 1 \}
                    \\,,
                    \\\
                    \\big\{
                    0 \rightrightarrows 1
                    \\big\}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \{ 0 \to 1\}
                    \\end{array}
                    \\right\}
                    \\,,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mn 0
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⟶
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mn 0
                              %mover
                                %mo ↔
                                %mo ∼
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    J
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left\{
                    \\array{
                    \\{ 0 \}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\{ 0 \xleftrightarrow{\sim} 1\}
                    }
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
            %p
          (
          %a{:href => "#Rezk96"}> Rezk 1996, Prop. 4.1
          )
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The functors in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              are evidently cofibrations. Hence by
              %a{:href => "cofibrantly+generated+model+category#RetractsOfCellComplexesExchaustLLPOfRLP"} this Prop
              we need to show that:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi RLP
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi I
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RLP(I)
                  is the class of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
                %p One sees immediately that:
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      right lifting against
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∅
                            %mo →
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to \{0\}
                      characterizes
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+on+objects+functors"} surjective on objects functors
                  %li
                    %p
                      right lifting against
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo ⊔
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo →
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \sqcup \{1\} \longrightarrow \{ 0 \to 1 \}
                      characterizes
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functors"} full functors
                  %li
                    %p
                      right lifting against
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ⇉
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo →
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{0 \rightrightarrows 1 \big\}\longrightarrow \{ 0 \to 1\}
                      characterizes
                      = succeed "." do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functors"} faithful functors
                %p
                  Together this characterizes the acyclic fibrations, by Rem.
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheAcyclicFibrations"}
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi RLP
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi J
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RLP(J)
                  is the class of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
                %p
                  But right lifting against
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mover
                          %mo ↔
                          %mo ∼
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \longrightarrow \{0 \xleftrightarrow{\sim} 1\}
                  is immediately the condition of path lifting which defines
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibrations"} isofibrations
            %p
          %p
          #CartesianMonoidalModelStructure.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (cartesian monoidal model structure)
              %br/
              The canonical model structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi can
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{can}
              (from Prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceOfTheCanonicalModelStructure"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+model+category"} cartesian monoidal model category
          (
          %a{:href => "#Rezk96"}> Rezk 1996, Thm. 5.1
          ,
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "#Joyal10"} Joyal 2010, Thm. 1.3
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Since forming
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+categories"} product categories
              with a fixed category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              evidently preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
              and hence the canonical weak equivalences, the
              %a{:href => "monoidal+model+category#UnitAxiom"} unit axiom
              follows immediately. It remains to check the
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "monoidal+model+category#PushoutProductAxiom"} pushout-product axiom
            %p
              So consider a diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              of this form:
              %svg{:height => "232.816pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 324.9 232.816", :width => "324.9pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
